WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.319 --> 00:00:22.880
The 5th century BC represented the pinnacle
of cultural, political, and economical power

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:24.320
for the city-state of Athens.

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:28.460
It was a true golden age that would go on
to have tremendous influence not only on the

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:31.289
Greek world, but on all of Western civilization.

00:00:31.289 --> 00:00:35.710
Socrates was laying down the foundations of
Western philosophy, aided by students such

00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:40.280
as Plato, as well as other prominent thinkers
of that time such as Anaxagoras, Empedocles,

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:41.280
and Democritus.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:43.610
In the arts, Athenian theater was the envy
of the Greek world thanks to talented playwrights

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:44.730
such as Euripides, Sophocles, Aristophanes,
and Aeschylus.

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:48.750
A man named Phidias cemented his position
as the best sculptor of his time when he made

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:53.160
the Statue of Zeus at Olympia, ranked among
the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:58.110
Not to mention the fact that almost all the
structures located on the Acropolis, most

00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:02.239
famously the Parthenon, were also built during
this time.

00:01:02.239 --> 00:01:06.530
Other Athenians thought that history was important
enough to be recorded for future generations.

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:11.430
Here we meet an old friend of Biographics,
Herodotus, as well as Thucydides and Xenophon.

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:15.920
The architect Hippodamus of Miletus was considered
one of the founders of urban planning while

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:20.000
Hippocrates pioneered western medicine and
gave his name to the Hippocratic Oath.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.670
All of these great people lived and worked
in one place, at one time.

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:26.560
And then, of course, there was Pericles.

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:31.510
Sometimes, this golden age of Athens is also
called the “Age of Pericles” because he

00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:33.780
was there to support and oversee it all.

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:38.880
He was the city’s leading statesman, a skilled
general, a patron of the arts, and an orator

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:39.880
without peers.

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:44.280
Under his leadership, Athens flourished almost
unlike any other city in history.

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:51.560
It is no surprise, then, that Pericles became
known as the “First Citizen of Athens.”

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:55.930
Early Years

00:01:55.930 --> 00:02:00.370
Pericles was born circa 495 BC, in Athens,
of course.

00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:04.560
His father was a wealthy politician named
Xanthippus, while his mother was named Agariste

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:08.729
and came from one of the oldest, most influential
families in the city - the Alcmaeonidae.

00:02:08.729 --> 00:02:12.730
According to Plutarch, (who will serve as
one of our primary ancient sources here) a

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:17.639
few days before Pericles was born, his mother
had a dream that she would give birth to a

00:02:17.639 --> 00:02:18.639
lion.

00:02:18.639 --> 00:02:22.299
Afterwards, the historian goes on to specify
that, even though Pericles’s personal appearance

00:02:22.299 --> 00:02:27.880
was “unimpeachable,” the Athenian apparently
had quite a large and elongated head and,

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:31.230
for this, he was often the butt of jokes of
Greek poets.

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:35.700
Plutarch says that Pericles’s head looked
like a squill, also known as a sea onion,

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:38.480
and that he was sometimes called “Schinocephalus”
or squill-head.

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:43.300
Besides the unusual shape of his cranium,
it was also said that Pericles greatly resembled

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:48.510
in appearance a man named Peisistratus, a
former tyrant of Athens who was not remembered

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:49.680
fondly in his time.

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:53.910
Therefore, as a youth, Pericles did not enjoy
speaking in public and, instead, focused on

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:57.440
his military career as he believed that that
would be his biggest calling.

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:01.669
It wasn’t until he got a bit older that
he became more eager to enter the world of

00:03:01.669 --> 00:03:02.669
politics.

00:03:02.669 --> 00:03:06.420
Given his family’s high status, it comes
as no surprise that young Pericles benefited

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:07.949
from a good education.

00:03:07.949 --> 00:03:12.530
Some of his known teachers included Pythocleides
who taught him music, Zeno who educated him

00:03:12.530 --> 00:03:17.760
on the natural world, and, his most beloved
tutor, Anaxagoras who told him about philosophy.

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:22.500
Despite his privileged childhood, Pericles
saw firsthand how fleeting power can be.

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:27.250
When he was around ten, his family’s status
was almost wiped out when his father, Xanthippus,

00:03:27.250 --> 00:03:29.090
was ostracized.

00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:33.540
Back then, ostracism didn’t just mean that
he got left out of Athenian politics, this

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:37.919
was an official procedure that resulted in
a 10-year exile from the city.

00:03:37.919 --> 00:03:42.419
However, in this case, it only lasted a few
years, until the Persians invaded Greece again.

00:03:42.419 --> 00:03:46.340
As one of Athens’ most capable military
leaders, Xanthippus was recalled in defense

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:47.340
of the city.

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:51.560
He acquitted himself very well when he led
the Athenian navy in the Battle of Mycale

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:53.799
in 479 BC.

00:03:53.799 --> 00:03:57.660
It was a decisive victory for the Greek cities
and, together with another triumph at Plataea,

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:00.199
marked the end of the second Persian invasion.

00:04:00.199 --> 00:04:05.040
Xanthippus only lived for a few more years
after that, but he died a hero of Athens and

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:11.329
ensured that his son would be welcomed into
the city’s political world with open arms.

00:04:11.329 --> 00:04:16.090
Athenian Politics

00:04:16.090 --> 00:04:20.970
Before we get into the details of how Pericles
rose to power, we should try to get some background

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:23.220
on the ruling political classes of Athens.

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:28.480
There are basically two groups that we need
to discuss: the archons and the strategoi.

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:33.040
The archons were the chief magistrates of
Athens and other Greek cities and they had

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:36.040
been around for hundreds of years before the
Age of Pericles.

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:40.260
Basically, whenever Athens was not ruled by
a king or a tyrant, it was ruled by these

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:48.000
archons who usually came in three: the eponymous
archon, the polemarch, and the archon basileus.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:52.091
The length of their office terms varied greatly
depending on the exact time period we’re

00:04:52.091 --> 00:04:53.091
talking about.

00:04:53.091 --> 00:04:57.150
At first, they ruled for life, then they were
limited to ten years and, finally, were restricted

00:04:57.150 --> 00:04:58.260
to a single year.

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:02.280
With the emergence of democracy, the power
and influence of the archons diminished greatly.

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:07.210
In fact, by the time of Pericles, the office
of archon was mainly ceremonial, with just

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:09.370
a few minor civic responsibilities.

00:05:09.370 --> 00:05:12.260
The authority was now in the hands of the
strategoi.

00:05:12.260 --> 00:05:17.720
Generally speaking, a strategos simply meant
a military leader; the ancient Greek version

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:18.720
of a general.

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:23.930
However, in 5th century Athens, there was
a board always consisting of ten strategoi

00:05:23.930 --> 00:05:27.650
who were in charge of all the important duties
of the city.

00:05:27.650 --> 00:05:31.470
While other positions like that of archon
were assigned by drawing lots, the strategoi

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:33.220
were elected every year.

00:05:33.220 --> 00:05:37.400
All strategoi were technically of equal rank
and made decisions through vote.

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:42.270
However, as you might expect, some were considerably
more influential than the rest and their opinion

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:44.690
carried a lot more weight.

00:05:44.690 --> 00:05:49.300
Rise to Power

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:54.680
Back to Pericles, he entered the political
world of Athens when he was in his early 20s.

00:05:54.680 --> 00:06:00.900
The earliest recorded act of his is dated
to 472 BC when he sponsored a play by Aeschylus

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:02.530
called The Persians.

00:06:02.530 --> 00:06:07.420
In this case, Pericles was a choregos - a
rich citizen of Athens who paid for the production

00:06:07.420 --> 00:06:09.740
of a play, whether he wanted to or not.

00:06:09.740 --> 00:06:14.430
These roles were usually assigned and, in
fact, represented one of the few duties still

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:19.460
performed by the archons but, in this case,
it is believed Pericles wanted to sponsor

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:22.260
this particular play to send a message.

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:26.600
Like we said, even though all strategoi were
equal in rank, some were “more equal”

00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:27.700
than the others.

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:32.570
At this time, the leadership of Athens was
divided between two factions, one led by Themistocles,

00:06:32.570 --> 00:06:33.890
the other by Cimon.

00:06:33.890 --> 00:06:38.200
The play sponsored by Pericles evoked the
Battle of Salamis where the Greeks led by

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:40.380
Themistocles defeated the Persians.

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:44.920
People regarded this as Pericles officially
siding with him in his power struggle against

00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:45.920
Cimon.

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:48.230
Unfortunately for Pericles, he chose poorly.

00:06:48.230 --> 00:06:53.340
The list of enemies for Themistocles was growing
larger and larger and it included all of Sparta

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:55.120
who were friendly with Cimon.

00:06:55.120 --> 00:07:00.190
Eventually, after multiple accusations, Themistocles
was ostracized and exiled.

00:07:00.190 --> 00:07:05.120
That wasn’t enough for Sparta, though, which
accused the former war hero of aiding a Spartan

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:10.900
conspiracy plot and wanted him tried by all
the Greek cities, not just Athens.

00:07:10.900 --> 00:07:14.700
Since this likely meant a death sentence,
Themistocles was forced to leave the whole

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:19.810
of Greece and, of all places, he ended up
in Persia which was now led by King Artaxerses

00:07:19.810 --> 00:07:23.990
I, the son of the man that Themistocles defeated
in battle.

00:07:23.990 --> 00:07:26.190
Fortunately for him, the king was not interested
in revenge.

00:07:26.190 --> 00:07:30.670
If anything, he was happy to have one of his
father’s most capable foes in his service.

00:07:30.670 --> 00:07:35.210
The exiled Greek relocated to the prosperous
city of Magnesia where he was named governor

00:07:35.210 --> 00:07:37.460
and spent the rest of his days.

00:07:37.460 --> 00:07:41.200
Back in Athens, Pericles was steadily growing
in power and winning over the people.

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:45.810
However, he still had a major obstacle in
the form of Cimon . Like Pericles, he was

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:47.460
the son of a beloved war hero.

00:07:47.460 --> 00:07:51.710
In this case, Miltiades, the man who led Greece
to victory at the Battle of Marathon.

00:07:51.710 --> 00:07:56.669
However, Cimon was also himself a distinguished
military veteran, known for his triumph at

00:07:56.669 --> 00:07:58.211
the Battle of the Eurymedon.

00:07:58.211 --> 00:08:02.199
He was also richer than Pericles, and could
afford to be more generous in his spending

00:08:02.199 --> 00:08:04.090
to win the affections of the public.

00:08:04.090 --> 00:08:09.169
It seemed like Cimon had the clear advantage,
but fortunes are often fleeting.

00:08:09.169 --> 00:08:13.860
All Pericles had to do was wait for the opportune
moment to strike against his opponent and

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:15.389
destroy his public standing.

00:08:15.389 --> 00:08:19.770
He first acted on some rumors that Cimon had
taken bribes from Alexander I of Macedon.

00:08:19.770 --> 00:08:21.580
No, not the famous one.

00:08:21.580 --> 00:08:24.880
Who knows, Pericles may have even started
the rumors himself.

00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:28.820
The important thing is that they allowed him
to formally charge his rival with bribery

00:08:28.820 --> 00:08:32.360
and, even though Cimon was acquitted, it still
attacked his credibility.

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:36.090
That being said, Cimon’s fall was ultimately
of his own doing.

00:08:36.090 --> 00:08:37.810
As we said, he was close to Sparta.

00:08:37.810 --> 00:08:42.490
In fact, he acted as their proxenos - sort
of an ambassador - and advocated for a close

00:08:42.490 --> 00:08:45.310
relationship between the two powerful Greek
states.

00:08:45.310 --> 00:08:52.080
In 462 BC, Sparta experienced a helot rebellion,
the helots being the lower class of Spartan

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:54.520
society, similar to medieval serfs.

00:08:54.520 --> 00:08:58.680
Cimon believed that sending soldiers to aid
Sparta would help cement the bond between

00:08:58.680 --> 00:08:59.680
them.

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:00.810
Other leaders were not as eager.

00:09:00.810 --> 00:09:04.730
With the Persians out of the picture, they
saw Sparta for what it truly was - their main

00:09:04.730 --> 00:09:06.730
competitor and biggest threat.

00:09:06.730 --> 00:09:11.200
Even so, Cimon convinced everyone and personally
led thousands of troops to Sparta.

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:15.210
When he arrived, though, he received a very
unpleasant surprise - the Spartans did not

00:09:15.210 --> 00:09:16.210
want them.

00:09:16.210 --> 00:09:20.320
In fact, the last thing they needed was for
the helots to learn about that damn Athenian

00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:21.320
democracy.

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:24.150
Cimon was turned back and had to return to
Athens completely humiliated.

00:09:24.150 --> 00:09:30.250
Afterwards, Pericles had no difficulty in
getting Cimon ostracized in 461 BC and exiled

00:09:30.250 --> 00:09:31.380
for ten years.

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:35.210
With the opposition gone, Pericles’s party
was the most powerful political entity in

00:09:35.210 --> 00:09:37.260
Athens, but it was not led by Pericles.

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:38.710
Not yet, anyway.

00:09:38.710 --> 00:09:43.040
It was led by its senior member and mentor
of Pericles, a man named Ephialtes.

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:47.310
He began enacting reforms to restrict the
power of Athenian oligarchs and to expand

00:09:47.310 --> 00:09:51.170
the democratic privileges of the citizens,
but he didn’t get to see his policies in

00:09:51.170 --> 00:09:54.020
action as he was assassinated that same year.

00:09:54.020 --> 00:09:59.480
Nobody knew who did the deed so, obviously,
enemies of Pericles accused the Athenian of

00:09:59.480 --> 00:10:02.040
killing his mentor to take over his position.

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:04.250
Such accusations were considered baseless.

00:10:04.250 --> 00:10:09.420
Plutarch, for example, said that they were
“hurled, as if so much venom, against one…who

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:15.140
had a noble disposition and an ambitious spirit,
wherein no such savage and bestial feelings

00:10:15.140 --> 00:10:16.660
can have their abode.”

00:10:16.660 --> 00:10:21.100
He also mentions, quoting Aristotle as his
source, that the killer of Ephialtes was a

00:10:21.100 --> 00:10:23.380
man named Aristodicus of Tanagra.

00:10:23.380 --> 00:10:29.540
Who he was, why he did it, and how Aristotle
came to learn of him, we have no idea.

00:10:29.540 --> 00:10:32.980
The point is that, with the leader of the
opposition exiled and the leader of his own

00:10:32.980 --> 00:10:38.440
party dead, Pericles was now able to consolidate
power and become the most powerful man in

00:10:38.440 --> 00:11:56.040
Athens, a position he would maintain until

00:11:56.040 --> 00:11:59.360
his death.

00:11:59.360 --> 00:12:08.300
Athens VS Sparta

00:12:08.300 --> 00:12:11.860
When Persia first began its invasion of the
Greek cities at the start of the 5th century

00:12:11.860 --> 00:12:16.680
BC, Sparta was the unofficial, but uncontested
leader of the Greek forces.

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:20.561
After Sparta came its close allies who were
all part of the Peloponnesian League, a group

00:12:20.561 --> 00:12:24.820
of city-states all located in or around the
Peloponnese Peninsula.

00:12:24.820 --> 00:12:27.450
These included Corinth, Elis, Pylos, Kythira,
and others.

00:12:27.450 --> 00:12:31.130
Finally, there were the rest of the Greek
states that would probably not have been allies

00:12:31.130 --> 00:12:34.250
if they were not fighting a common foe, the
Persians.

00:12:34.250 --> 00:12:37.980
These last states came to resent Sparta, mainly
due to two factors.

00:12:37.980 --> 00:12:42.310
For starters, naval warfare ended up becoming
a very important factor in the wars.

00:12:42.310 --> 00:12:47.110
Sparta did not have a strong navy, not as
powerful as Athens, for instance, or Argos,

00:12:47.110 --> 00:12:48.970
or Chalcis, or Chios.

00:12:48.970 --> 00:12:52.750
Therefore, these naval powers weren’t happy
that their status in the alliance didn’t

00:12:52.750 --> 00:12:55.720
quite match up to the contribution they were
bringing to the war.

00:12:55.720 --> 00:12:59.020
Even more contentious, though, was the idea
of a Greek counterattack.

00:12:59.020 --> 00:13:04.220
After the victories at Mycale and Plataea,
the invasion of mainland Greece was over and,

00:13:04.220 --> 00:13:07.029
as far as Sparta was concerned, so was the
war.

00:13:07.029 --> 00:13:09.870
The Greeks won, the Persians left, job done.

00:13:09.870 --> 00:13:14.100
But other city-states like Athens wanted to
keep the pressure on and go into Asia Minor

00:13:14.100 --> 00:13:18.760
and liberate the Ionian Greek states which
were still under Persian control and had been

00:13:18.760 --> 00:13:22.820
for almost a hundred years since they were
conquered by Cyrus the Great.

00:13:22.820 --> 00:13:27.090
At this point, Sparta and its close allies
withdrew from the war and the leadership of

00:13:27.090 --> 00:13:29.680
the remaining Greek states was passed on to
Athens.

00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:34.330
In 478 BC, they even formed their own association
called the Delian League.

00:13:34.330 --> 00:13:39.050
The name came from the island of Delos which
acted as a neutral meeting point for all the

00:13:39.050 --> 00:13:41.390
members, and also served as the league’s
treasury.

00:13:41.390 --> 00:13:45.210
Even in ancient times, historians such as
Thucydides regarded the counterattack on the

00:13:45.210 --> 00:13:49.560
Persian Empire as just a pretext to form this
league without opposition.

00:13:49.560 --> 00:13:54.080
The main goal was to protect its members from
all future enemies and, although never stated,

00:13:54.080 --> 00:13:57.800
it was clearly intended to counteract Sparta’s
Peloponnesian League.

00:13:57.800 --> 00:14:03.140
From this point on, Athens’ power and influence
in the Greek world steadily grew to the displeasure

00:14:03.140 --> 00:14:04.140
of Sparta.

00:14:04.140 --> 00:14:07.641
Over the following decades, the relationship
between the two states became tense, even

00:14:07.641 --> 00:14:09.870
though it was still outwardly friendly.

00:14:09.870 --> 00:14:14.510
One important moment came when Athens rebuilt
its walls in secret under the direction of

00:14:14.510 --> 00:14:15.870
Themistocles.

00:14:15.870 --> 00:14:19.600
Sparta insisted that no Greek cities should
build walls, under the pretense that they

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:23.590
would provide fortifications for a foreign
invader if they took over the city.

00:14:23.590 --> 00:14:27.810
Of course, what others took this to mean was
that Sparta preferred if the rest of the Greek

00:14:27.810 --> 00:14:29.990
states stayed reliant on them for protection.

00:14:29.990 --> 00:14:34.399
Well, Athens didn’t listen to Sparta, and
not only did they build their walls, they

00:14:34.399 --> 00:14:39.390
also declared themselves free of Spartan hegemony,
capable of defending themselves.

00:14:39.390 --> 00:14:43.950
Over the years, the Delian League slowly,
but surely turned into an unofficial Athenian

00:14:43.950 --> 00:14:44.950
Empire.

00:14:44.950 --> 00:14:48.399
Since its inception, each member was expected
to provide an annual tribute in the form of

00:14:48.399 --> 00:14:50.670
either money or soldiers and weapons.

00:14:50.670 --> 00:14:55.410
With no immediate wars on the horizon, most
members chose to pay, but Athens instead kept

00:14:55.410 --> 00:14:57.990
adding soldiers to the forces of the Delian
League.

00:14:57.990 --> 00:15:01.690
After a couple of decades, the bulk of the
league’s army consisted almost entirely

00:15:01.690 --> 00:15:07.120
of Athenian soldiers so, in reality, the other
members were basically paying Athens to keep

00:15:07.120 --> 00:15:08.120
them safe.

00:15:08.120 --> 00:15:11.631
This continued under Pericles who was eager
to keep increasing the hegemony of Athens

00:15:11.631 --> 00:15:12.631
over the Delian League.

00:15:12.631 --> 00:15:17.170
At one point, he refused to accept any more
soldiers or supplies as tribute, instead insisting

00:15:17.170 --> 00:15:18.470
that everyone pay money.

00:15:18.470 --> 00:15:23.430
The true turning point, however, occurred
in 454 BC, when Pericles moved the treasury

00:15:23.430 --> 00:15:26.810
of the Delian League from the island of Delos
to Athens.

00:15:26.810 --> 00:15:30.760
He did it under the pretense that the money
was safer in Athens but, in reality, he used

00:15:30.760 --> 00:15:32.610
it to fund his own building projects.

00:15:32.610 --> 00:15:37.350
This was bad for them, but great for us, and
great for history as a whole because Pericles’s

00:15:37.350 --> 00:15:41.649
most ambitious construction plan was to rebuild
the Acropolis of Athens.

00:15:41.649 --> 00:15:44.180
Thanks to him, the city got marvelous structures
such as the Propylaea, the Temple of Athena

00:15:44.180 --> 00:15:45.180
Nike, and, of course, the Parthenon .

00:15:45.180 --> 00:15:47.050
His spending ways were not always well-received
in Athens.

00:15:47.050 --> 00:15:51.510
In fact, they often represented the main criticism
of Pericles by his political opponents.

00:15:51.510 --> 00:15:55.740
During the 440s, the other political party
started gaining power again, led by a man

00:15:55.740 --> 00:15:58.910
named Thucydides, but not to be confused with
the historian.

00:15:58.910 --> 00:16:03.770
The statesman wanted to have Pericles ostracized
for playing fast and loose with public funds

00:16:03.770 --> 00:16:06.510
and, for a while, got the Athenian people
on his side.

00:16:06.510 --> 00:16:11.649
However, in an example of supreme oratorical
skill, Pericles managed to win back the public.

00:16:11.649 --> 00:16:16.220
He even said that he would pay for the construction
from his own pocket, as long as the inscriptions

00:16:16.220 --> 00:16:17.519
were dedicated to him alone.

00:16:17.519 --> 00:16:20.910
The magnanimity of his offer was enough to
sway the Athenians.

00:16:20.910 --> 00:16:26.170
Public spending continued as before, Thucydides
was himself ostracized and exiled, and Pericles

00:16:26.170 --> 00:16:28.899
stayed in power.

00:16:28.899 --> 00:16:33.560
The First Peloponnesian War

00:16:33.560 --> 00:16:37.120
Ever since Athens started gaining power and
influence in the Greek world, it was only

00:16:37.120 --> 00:16:41.610
a matter of time before it would collide with
Sparta which did not want any other city-state

00:16:41.610 --> 00:16:42.779
to rival its strength.

00:16:42.779 --> 00:16:48.040
The year 460 BC saw the outbreak of the First
Peloponnesian War between Athens and Sparta,

00:16:48.040 --> 00:16:49.180
as well as their allies.

00:16:49.180 --> 00:16:53.079
Just for added clarity, though, this is not
the same as the more famous conflict known

00:16:53.079 --> 00:16:56.660
simply as the Peloponnesian War, which we
will talk about in a bit.

00:16:56.660 --> 00:17:00.060
Technically, that should have been named the
Second Peloponnesian War, but because it was

00:17:00.060 --> 00:17:04.959
much grander, lasted longer and had much more
significant historical consequences, it became

00:17:04.959 --> 00:17:07.180
known just as the Peloponnesian War.

00:17:07.180 --> 00:17:11.679
Ignoring the underlying tensions in the Greek
world, this war ostensibly broke out due to

00:17:11.679 --> 00:17:14.339
a border dispute between Megara and Corinth.

00:17:14.339 --> 00:17:20.109
In 460 BC, Megara left the Peloponnesian League
and requested to join the Delian League.

00:17:20.109 --> 00:17:24.069
This caused conflict between Athens and Corinth
who were the initial belligerents of the First

00:17:24.069 --> 00:17:27.639
Peloponnesian War, with Sparta only intervening
on occasion.

00:17:27.639 --> 00:17:32.110
Pericles was reluctant to engage in an all-out
battle with the Peloponnese forces because

00:17:32.110 --> 00:17:36.710
his army was spread far and wide, still aiding
other nations in Asia Minor and Egypt who

00:17:36.710 --> 00:17:38.710
were rebelling against the Persians.

00:17:38.710 --> 00:17:43.779
He engaged the Corinthians several times and,
eventually, managed to drive them out of Megara.

00:17:43.779 --> 00:17:47.289
Meanwhile, Sparta spent the first few years
of the war avoiding Athens.

00:17:47.289 --> 00:17:51.769
Instead, they battled Phocis, an Athenian
ally, who themselves were in conflict with

00:17:51.769 --> 00:17:53.450
the neighboring state of Doris.

00:17:53.450 --> 00:17:57.799
Their goal appeared to be to conquer or submit
all the city-states in the region of Boeotia

00:17:57.799 --> 00:17:59.889
and unify them all under one leadership.

00:17:59.889 --> 00:18:06.080
It wasn’t until 457 BC that Sparta and Athens
finally met in battle at Tanagra.

00:18:06.080 --> 00:18:11.210
According to Thucydides, the Spartans won,
but “the slaughter was great on both sides”

00:18:11.210 --> 00:18:14.609
so they could not press on the attack and
had to retreat.

00:18:14.609 --> 00:18:18.570
The Athenians, however, managed to regroup
and, just a few months later, soundly defeated

00:18:18.570 --> 00:18:21.970
all the Spartan allies in the area at the
Battle of Oenophyta, putting a stop to Sparta’s

00:18:21.970 --> 00:18:23.879
plan of consolidating power in Boeotia.

00:18:23.879 --> 00:18:29.649
Pericles had the advantage, but it went away
in 454 BC thanks to the Persians.

00:18:29.649 --> 00:18:33.299
Like we previously mentioned, a large part
of the Athenian army was still assisting other

00:18:33.299 --> 00:18:36.730
nations trying to escape the subjugation of
the Achaemenid Empire.

00:18:36.730 --> 00:18:41.919
The Delian League had sent 200 ships to Egypt
to help a rebel leader called Inarus.

00:18:41.919 --> 00:18:46.570
The expedition ended in disaster for Athens
following the Siege of Prosopis, as most of

00:18:46.570 --> 00:18:49.980
their fleet was destroyed, while Inarus was
captured and crucified.

00:18:49.980 --> 00:18:54.889
This may have forced a peace treaty between
Athens and Persia known as the Peace of Callias.

00:18:54.889 --> 00:18:59.730
More or less, it said that Persia would leave
the Greek cities in Asia Minor alone, but

00:18:59.730 --> 00:19:01.390
Athens had to stay out of North Africa.

00:19:01.390 --> 00:19:05.509
The disaster of the Egyptian campaign had
serious repercussions for Athens as it lost

00:19:05.509 --> 00:19:06.799
its naval supremacy.

00:19:06.799 --> 00:19:10.859
Not only was it threatened by the Peloponnesian
League, but also by Greek cities from the

00:19:10.859 --> 00:19:13.049
Delian League who wanted to rebel.

00:19:13.049 --> 00:19:18.269
This culminated in another defeat at the Battle
of Coronea in 447 BC against all the Boeotian

00:19:18.269 --> 00:19:21.289
city-states that Athens had previously submitted.

00:19:21.289 --> 00:19:25.619
From this point on, Pericles decided mostly
to stay away from mainland Greece affairs

00:19:25.619 --> 00:19:29.769
and focus his efforts on the Aegean where
Athens was most dominant.

00:19:29.769 --> 00:19:33.980
A surprise appearance here was made by Cimon,
once the main political rival of Pericles.

00:19:33.980 --> 00:19:37.789
His exile was up so he returned to Athens
in 451 BC.

00:19:37.789 --> 00:19:41.970
Still on good terms with Sparta, he first
negotiated a temporary truce between the two

00:19:41.970 --> 00:19:47.769
powers which, eventually, culminated in the
Thirty Years Peace of 446 BC, thus ending

00:19:47.769 --> 00:19:53.220
the First Peloponnesian War.

00:19:53.220 --> 00:19:56.231
The Age of Pericles Ends

00:19:56.231 --> 00:19:58.820
Don’t be fooled by the name of the Thirty
Years’ Peace.

00:19:58.820 --> 00:20:02.009
It did not last three decades and nobody was
expecting it to.

00:20:02.009 --> 00:20:06.289
Over the next 15 years, Athens and Sparta
continued to act aggressively to one another,

00:20:06.289 --> 00:20:10.929
but they did it by proxy by finding cause
to attack each other’s allies.

00:20:10.929 --> 00:20:15.289
The most notable example of this was the Samian
War of 440 BC.

00:20:15.289 --> 00:20:18.979
Athens intervened in a conflict between Samos
and Miletus.

00:20:18.979 --> 00:20:23.799
Even though Samos was a member of the Delian
League, the city reportedly asked Sparta for

00:20:23.799 --> 00:20:24.799
help.

00:20:24.799 --> 00:20:28.830
The Spartans seriously considered going to
war against Athens again, but backed out when

00:20:28.830 --> 00:20:33.600
Corinth would not join them since they needed
the Corinthian fleet as the only naval power

00:20:33.600 --> 00:20:34.999
of the Peloponnesian League.

00:20:34.999 --> 00:20:39.169
Strangely, the same cities that were involved
in the outbreak of the First Peloponnesian

00:20:39.169 --> 00:20:40.960
War again played important roles.

00:20:40.960 --> 00:20:46.029
Megara, which was, once more, an ally of Sparta,
had angered Athens by desecrating one of its

00:20:46.029 --> 00:20:47.029
holy sites.

00:20:47.029 --> 00:20:51.729
In reply, Pericles issued the Megarian Decree,
banning Megarian ships and merchants from

00:20:51.729 --> 00:20:54.100
all markets and ports of the Delian League.

00:20:54.100 --> 00:20:57.749
It was the ancient Athenian version of a trade
embargo.

00:20:57.749 --> 00:21:01.950
Meanwhile, Athens also got involved in a dispute
between Corinth and one of its colonies named

00:21:01.950 --> 00:21:04.820
Corcyra, today known as Corfu.

00:21:04.820 --> 00:21:09.389
Conversely, Corinth secretly aided a different
colony named Potidaea with soldiers and weapons

00:21:09.389 --> 00:21:11.200
in a battle against Athens.

00:21:11.200 --> 00:21:15.070
These were all considered to go against the
terms of the Thirty Years’ Peace and, in

00:21:15.070 --> 00:21:20.700
431 BC, the Peloponnesian League led by Spartan
King Archidamus II declared war on Athens

00:21:20.700 --> 00:21:21.700
once more.

00:21:21.700 --> 00:21:24.980
Pericles’s strategy was to play to his strengths
as much as possible.

00:21:24.980 --> 00:21:28.489
Athens dominated the waters, Sparta was unmatched
on land.

00:21:28.489 --> 00:21:33.779
He knew that the enemy would come to ravage
his territories, but decided to avoid an open

00:21:33.779 --> 00:21:34.779
battle.

00:21:34.779 --> 00:21:38.220
Instead, he relocated his people to the safety
of Athens itself and, while the Spartan army

00:21:38.220 --> 00:21:42.220
was pillaging his farms, he took his navy
and plundered the Peloponnesian coasts.

00:21:42.220 --> 00:21:46.950
Unfortunately, we cannot see the Peloponnesian
War to its conclusion because, well, neither

00:21:46.950 --> 00:21:47.950
did Pericles.

00:21:47.950 --> 00:21:52.870
In 429 BC, just two years after the start
of the war, the mighty statesman and half

00:21:52.870 --> 00:21:56.419
his family were killed by a plague that ravaged
the city of Athens.

00:21:56.419 --> 00:22:01.419
The Peloponnesian War lasted for another 25
years and resulted in a definitive Spartan

00:22:01.419 --> 00:22:14.379
victory, thus bringing an end to the Golden
Age of Athens, the Age

00:22:14.379 --> 00:22:20.889
of Pericles.

